WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:09.765
Damn!

00:00:10.969 --> 00:00:14.948
Bleacher Report is innovative because we get to talk about sports

00:00:14.953 --> 00:00:16.483
like the fans that we are

00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:20.175
We're not inserting ourselves into the conversation,

00:00:20.181 --> 00:00:21.332
we are the conversation

00:00:21.337 --> 00:00:22.756
We are the sauce,

00:00:22.761 --> 00:00:24.246
we're swagu. All day

00:00:24.407 --> 00:00:27.160
At B/R, we come up with an idea, we brainstorm,

00:00:27.143 --> 00:00:29.051
and then within a couple of hours

00:00:29.051 --> 00:00:31.164
it’s across all of B/R’s platforms

00:00:33.772 --> 00:00:36.725
It really comes down to us just figuring out what is

00:00:36.642 --> 00:00:39.194
the best possible journey for the user to really

00:00:39.194 --> 00:00:42.219
drive the conversation around those moments that matter

00:00:42.225 --> 00:00:46.068
Anything that we think about that can resonate with our audience

00:00:46.073 --> 00:00:49.916
that has ties to pop culture, sports, entertainment, anything

00:00:49.922 --> 00:00:52.407
that our audience and we think is cool

00:00:52.391 --> 00:00:54.498
and we can create, we will

00:00:54.498 --> 00:00:55.498
You can walk

00:00:55.498 --> 00:00:57.635
You wanna see what my life is?

00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.770
B/R tells stories that stick to your bones

00:00:59.776 --> 00:01:02.484
The kind of stuff you want to share in your group chat

00:01:02.484 --> 00:01:04.795
or pass your cellphone to your friend,

00:01:04.792 --> 00:01:06.063
and be like "yo did you see this?" 

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:09.978
B/R came to us and we pitched them on Game of Zones,

00:01:09.978 --> 00:01:12.569
which is a mashup of "Game of Thrones" and the NBA

00:01:12.558 --> 00:01:14.182
And Bleacher Report said, “Great” and soon 

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:15.417
it was all over the internet

00:01:15.417 --> 00:01:17.299
It's game time!

00:01:18.139 --> 00:01:21.322
It allowed us to really push the way that we

00:01:21.331 --> 00:01:23.380
and everyone else are talking about sports,

00:01:23.377 --> 00:01:26.450
through cartoons, and through humor and through

00:01:26.457 --> 00:01:28.040
a different form of storytelling

00:01:28.129 --> 00:01:30.348
There are no rules now!

00:01:30.637 --> 00:01:32.903
So I started an Instagram account called House of Highlights

00:01:32.861 --> 00:01:35.506
and after a few months, I was gaining some attention

00:01:35.485 --> 00:01:36.485
and B/R recognized that

00:01:36.507 --> 00:01:38.131
They thought what I was doing was unique and different

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:39.130
They brought me in

00:01:38.929 --> 00:01:40.978
They believed in me. They got out of the way,

00:01:40.997 --> 00:01:42.847
and they said “hey Omar we want you to be yourself"

00:01:42.842 --> 00:01:44.448
And that really helped bring House of Highlights

00:01:44.443 --> 00:01:45.294
to that next level

00:01:45.294 --> 00:01:46.294
What are you doing?

00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:50.210
At B/R, we own the moments

00:01:50.202 --> 00:01:51.833
and our audience recognizes that

00:01:51.833 --> 00:01:53.124
When you're sitting with your group of friends,

00:01:53.368 --> 00:01:56.616
and somebody asks you, “Did you see that game last night?”

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.308
Somebody is going to say, “Did you see what Bleacher Report did?”

00:01:59.474 --> 00:02:01.399
That’s the thing that gets people talking,

00:02:01.698 --> 00:02:03.467
that’s the thing people really want to see,

00:02:03.765 --> 00:02:05.291
that’s the difference between us

00:02:05.833 --> 00:02:07.160
and everybody else

